## Phase 0 — Code Preparation (Local, No DO Yet)

Do these **before** creating anything on DigitalOcean.

### 0.1 — Verify CORS is Dynamic
Check `logihub/backend/main.py` — CORS origins must load from an env var (already done in a previous session, just verify):
```python
cors_origins = os.getenv("CORS_ORIGINS", "http://localhost:3000").split(",")
```

### 0.2 — Verify Backend Reads All Config from Env Vars
`logihub/backend/env.py` (or config.py) must read:
- `DATABASE_URL` → will be Supabase PostgreSQL URL
- `REDIS_URL` → will be Upstash Redis URL
- `JWT_SECRET`, `BOT_SECRET`, `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN`, `CORS_ORIGINS`

No hardcoded connection strings.

### 0.3 — Check Backend HTTP Port
App Platform web services expect port **8080** by default.
Two options — pick one:
- Change `uvicorn` to `--port 8080` in your Dockerfile CMD
- OR set `PORT=8000` as an env var in the App Platform UI and ensure uvicorn uses `$PORT`

The current Dockerfile uses `8000` — the simplest fix is to set `PORT=8000` in App Platform.

### 0.4 — Telegram Bot: Keep Polling Mode
The bot runs as a **Worker** service (no HTTP port). Polling mode (`await dp.start_polling(bot)`) is correct for this setup — no webhook changes needed.

### 0.5 — Commit Everything Clean
```bash
git add .
git commit -m "chore: prep for DO App Platform deploy"
git push origin main
```

---

## Phase 1 — Set Up Free External Services

### 1.1 — Supabase PostgreSQL (Free)
1. Go to [supabase.com](https://supabase.com) → Sign up free
2. Click **New Project** → set a strong DB password → pick region closest to users
3. Go to **Settings → Database → Connection string → URI (with pooler)**
4. The URL format: `postgresql+asyncpg://postgres.[ref]:[password]@aws-0-[region].pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres`
5. Save this URL — you'll paste it into DO as `DATABASE_URL`

### 1.2 — Upstash Redis (Free)
1. Go to [upstash.com](https://upstash.com) → Sign up free
2. **Create Database** → Redis → pick region → free tier
3. Copy the **REST URL** or the `rediss://` connection URL
4. Save as `REDIS_URL`

---

## Phase 2 — Create the DigitalOcean App

### 2.1 — Start a New App
1. Login → [cloud.digitalocean.com](https://cloud.digitalocean.com)
2. Click **Create → Apps**
3. **Source:** Connect to GitHub → authorize → select `LogicHub-Vibe` repo → branch `main`
4. DO will scan the repo and auto-detect Dockerfiles

### 2.2 — Configure Backend Service

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Component name | `logihub-backend` |
| Source directory | `logihub/backend` |
| Component type | **Web Service** |
| Dockerfile | `Dockerfile` (auto-detected) |
| HTTP port | `8000` |
| Plan | **Basic · $5/mo (512MB RAM)** |
| Instance count | 1 |

**Environment Variables** (mark all as **Encrypted**):
```
DATABASE_URL        = postgresql+asyncpg://postgres...[Supabase URL]
REDIS_URL           = rediss://...[Upstash URL]
JWT_SECRET          = [openssl rand -base64 32]
BOT_SECRET          = [openssl rand -base64 32]
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN  = [from @BotFather]
CORS_ORIGINS        = https://logihub-frontend.ondigitalocean.app
```

> Note: At this point you don't know the frontend URL yet. Set a placeholder — you'll update it in Phase 3.

### 2.3 — Deploy Backend First

Click **Save** and let the backend deploy. Once deployed, copy its URL:
`https://logihub-backend-xxxxx.ondigitalocean.app`

You need this URL before you can configure the frontend.

### 2.4 — Configure Frontend Service

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Component name | `logihub-frontend` |
| Source directory | `logihub/frontend` |
| Component type | **Web Service** |
| Dockerfile | `Dockerfile` (auto-detected) |
| HTTP port | `3000` |
| Plan | **Basic · $5/mo (512MB RAM)** |

**Environment Variables** (set BEFORE first build — Next.js bakes `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` vars at build time):
```
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL = https://logihub-backend-xxxxx.ondigitalocean.app
NODE_ENV            = production
```

### 2.5 — Configure Bot Worker Service

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Component name | `logihub-bot` |
| Source directory | `logihub/bot` |
| Component type | **Worker** (no HTTP port) |
| Dockerfile | `Dockerfile` |
| Plan | **Basic · $5/mo** |

**Environment Variables:**
```
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN = [from @BotFather]
BACKEND_URL        = https://logihub-backend-xxxxx.ondigitalocean.app
BOT_SECRET         = [same as backend]
```

### 2.6 — Review and Launch
1. App Platform shows cost summary — verify it shows ~$15/month
2. Click **Create Resources**
3. Watch build logs per service — first build: 5-10 minutes each

---

## Phase 3 — Post-Deploy Configuration

### 3.1 — Update CORS After Frontend URL Is Known
Once both are deployed:
1. Go to **Backend service → Environment Variables**
2. Update `CORS_ORIGINS` to the exact frontend URL
3. Click **Save** → backend auto-restarts

### 3.2 — Verify Migrations Ran
1. App Platform → **Backend** → **Console** tab
2. Run: `alembic -c migrations/alembic.ini upgrade head`
3. Or check startup logs — the Dockerfile CMD should have run this already

### 3.3 — Test End-to-End
- [ ] Frontend loads at its `.ondigitalocean.app` URL
- [ ] API responds: `https://logihub-backend-xxx.ondigitalocean.app/health`
- [ ] Login works (JWT auth)
- [ ] Real-time dashboard (WebSocket at `/ws/dashboard`) updates
- [ ] Telegram bot responds to messages

### 3.4 — WebSocket Support
App Platform's HTTP proxy supports WebSocket upgrades natively — no extra config needed.
Your FastAPI `/ws/dashboard` endpoint will work as-is.

---

## Phase 4 — CI/CD (Built-In, Zero Config)

Every push to `main` automatically triggers a rolling deploy. No YAML files needed.

```
git push origin main
     │
     ▼
GitHub webhook → App Platform
     │
     ▼
Build new Docker images (parallel)
     │
     ▼
Rolling replace old containers (zero downtime)
```

If you want a staging environment: duplicate the App, point it to a `staging` branch.

---

## Phase 5 — Monitoring

### 5.1 — Built-in App Platform Monitoring
- CPU, RAM, network per service → App Platform dashboard
- Real-time logs → **Runtime Logs** tab per service
- Alerts → **Monitoring → Create Alert** (CPU > 80%, error spikes)

### 5.2 — External Uptime Monitoring (Free)
[UptimeRobot](https://uptimerobot.com) — free, checks every 5 minutes:
- Monitor: `https://logihub-backend-xxxxx.ondigitalocean.app/health`
- Monitor: `https://logihub-frontend-xxxxx.ondigitalocean.app`
- Sends email or Telegram alert on downtime

### 5.3 — Database Backups
Supabase free tier includes daily backups + 7-day point-in-time recovery. No setup needed.

